course: biology
lesson: 297
title: The future of life: de-extinction, climate, coral and frozen zoos
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Scientists are trying new ways to look after life on Earth: freezing the cells of rare animals, building bridges for deer over roads, growing meat without farms, and even making elephants that look a little like mammoths.
explain: In Jurassic Park, scientists find dinosaur blood in a mosquito trapped in amber and bring T. rex back. It is a great story, but DNA breaks down over time, and after 66 million years no usable dinosaur DNA is left.
explain: The planet is also changing: as places warm, many plants and animals move towards the poles or up mountains, like children shifting along a bench into the shade on a hot day, until someone at the very end has nowhere left to go.
explain: Nobody knows exactly what life will look like in millions of years, but the next hundred years depend a lot on what people choose to do now.
fact: A company is trying to add mammoth genes to Asian elephant cells to make a cold-adapted elephant; it would be a new kind of elephant, not a true mammoth, and in 2025 it was reported to have made woolly mice with long golden hair as a test.
fact: In 2025 a company was reported to have made wolf pups with some genes changed to look like the extinct dire wolf; they are grey wolves with white fur and a few changes, and scientists disagree about calling it de-extinction.
fact: T. rex cannot be cloned, because cloning needs DNA and dinosaur DNA is long gone; birds are living dinosaurs, so a bird is the closest we can get.
fact: As places warm, many plants and animals move towards the poles or up mountains, and some cannot move fast enough or have nowhere higher to go.
fact: Corals turn white, which is called bleaching, when the water is too hot and they push out the tiny algae living in them, which get a home in the coral and make food from sunlight for it; without the algae they starve, though they can recover if the heat passes.
fact: Scientists breed tougher corals and plant them back on damaged reefs.
fact: Frozen zoos keep cells, eggs and sperm of rare animals in liquid nitrogen, which may help to breed more animals later; the northern white rhino, with only two females left, may be helped this way.
fact: In 2022 countries agreed on a goal called 30 by 30: to protect 30 percent of land and sea by 2030, which needs money and work with local people.
fact: Green bridges are bridges over roads covered with soil and plants; they let deer, bears and other animals cross safely, and in Canada's Banff National Park they cut the number of animals killed on the road sharply; on Christmas Island, where millions of red crabs cross the roads each year, the crabs have their own small bridges so they are not squashed.
fact: Scientists can grow meat from animal cells in tanks without raising whole animals; it is sold in a few countries, it could use less land, but it is still expensive.
fact: Scientists look for life on Mars and on moons such as Europa and Enceladus, which have oceans under ice; on Earth life survives in boiling vents and frozen lakes, but no life beyond Earth has been found yet.
fact: No one knows what animals will look like in millions of years, because evolution follows changes nobody can predict; after every great extinction in the past, life made new kinds.
exercise: What is a company trying to make by adding mammoth genes to elephant cells? | A cold-adapted elephant, not a true mammoth | An exact copy of a real ice age mammoth, hair and all | A tiny mammoth the size of a cat | A mammoth grown back from frozen meat
exercise: What test animals with long golden hair were reported in 2025? | Woolly mice | Woolly rabbits | Woolly goldfish | Woolly chickens
exercise: What were the «dire wolf» pups of 2025 really? | Grey wolves with some genes changed to look like dire wolves | Real dire wolves grown back from old fossil bones in a laboratory | Husky dogs painted white | Wolves cloned from a frozen dire wolf
exercise: Why can T. rex not be cloned? | Cloning needs DNA, and dinosaur DNA is long gone | It is against the law in every country of the world | T. rex was too big to fit into any laboratory on Earth | Its bones are kept in museums
exercise: Which living animals are dinosaurs themselves? | Birds | Lizards | Snakes | Frogs
exercise: Where do many animals and plants move as the climate warms? | Towards the poles or up mountains | Towards the equator, where it is hotter | Deeper into the hottest deserts | Into caves to sleep until it cools
exercise: Why does coral turn white when the sea is too hot? | It pushes out the tiny algae that live in it and feed it | The hot sun bleaches it white, just like an old T-shirt on a washing line | Salt builds up on top of it | Fish nibble off all its colour when they get hungry
exercise: Can bleached coral recover? | Yes, if the heat passes before it starves | No, bleached coral is always dead at once | Yes, but only if painted again | No, it turns into sand within a day
exercise: What does a frozen zoo keep? | Cells, eggs and sperm of rare animals in liquid nitrogen | Living animals kept in very cold cages so that they never age | Ice sculptures of extinct animals | Frozen food for zoo animals
exercise: Which animal, with only two females left, might be helped by frozen cells? | The northern white rhino | The African elephant | The red fox of the woods | The common house sparrow
exercise: What does the 30 by 30 goal mean? | Protect 30 percent of land and sea by 2030 | Plant 30 trees for each person on Earth by 2030 | Save 30 species in 30 years | Build 30 new zoos in every country of the world
exercise: What is a green bridge? | A bridge over a road covered with plants for animals to cross | A bridge painted bright green all over so that drivers slow down | A bridge made only from wood | A bridge for cyclists and walkers in big city parks
exercise: What is lab-grown meat? | Meat grown from animal cells in tanks | Meat made entirely from plants | Meat from animals raised in laboratories | Meat printed from plastic
exercise: Why do scientists look for life on Europa and Enceladus? | They have oceans of water under their ice | They are covered in thick green forests and lakes | They are the hottest moons | Radio signals from aliens were found coming from there
exercise: Has life been found anywhere beyond Earth? | Not yet | Yes, small animals on Mars | Yes, fish on Europa | Yes, plants on the Moon
exercise: What will animals look like in millions of years? | No one knows, because evolution cannot be predicted | Exactly like animals today, because nothing changes | They will all look like robots | They will all slowly turn back into dinosaurs again
test: «saw Jurassic Park, they got the DNA from a mosquito in amber, so we could totally make a T. rex» What is right? | No, after 66 million years the DNA is broken and gone | Yes, scientists already have one in a lab, it's just kept secret | Yes, any mosquito in amber has dino DNA | No, because T. rex never existed
test: «they brought back the dire wolf!!! it's real!!» What is the best answer? | They made grey wolves with a few changed genes; scientists argue if that counts | Yes, the exact ice age animal is alive again, grown from a fossil tooth | No, nothing was made at all | Yes, and it was dug up alive from the ice
test: A small mountain mouse lives near the top of a mountain. What could happen as the climate warms? | It may move up until there is nowhere higher left to go | It will grow wings and fly away to a colder place | Nothing, mountain tops never warm | It will move down to the warm valley, where it likes the heat
test: Coral and its tiny algae are partners. Why does each need the other? | The algae get a home, and they make food from sunlight for the coral | The coral eats the algae and gives nothing back to them | The algae paint the coral bright colours to scare away hungry fish | They do not need each other at all
test: Count them: a green bridge camera films 2 bears and 3 deer crossing. How many legs and how many tails? | 20 legs and 5 tails | 10 legs and 5 tails | 20 legs and 10 tails | 16 legs and 5 tails
test: Why keep frozen cells of animals that are still alive today? | So that if they become very rare, their cells can help breed more | Because frozen animals are easier to feed and look after in zoos than live ones | To sell them as ice cream | Because living animals do not need help
test: In a cartoon a woolly mammoth and a sabre-toothed cat are best friends in the ice age. If a company made a «mammoth» today, what would it really be? | An elephant with some mammoth genes, a new animal | The same mammoth from the cartoon | A real sabre-toothed cat | A big hairy cow
test: «why would anyone build a bridge for crabs lol» What is the reason? | Millions of red crabs cross roads on Christmas Island; bridges stop them being squashed | Crabs like to walk high up so they can see the sea from far away | To keep crabs out of the forest | Crabs pay to use the bridge
test: What would happen if a country protected 30 percent of its land and sea? | Many animals would have more safe homes, if the protection is real and paid for | Every animal in the country would move into the protected part at once | No animal could live outside it | Hunting would end everywhere in the world
test: «lab meat? gross, it's just plastic» What is true? | It is real meat grown from animal cells, but still expensive | It is made from plastic and paint, so it can't be eaten by anyone | It is meat from robot animals | It is only a trick done for films
test: Where on Earth does life survive that gives scientists hope for life in space? | Boiling vents in the deep sea and frozen lakes | Only warm, sunny meadows full of flowers and bees | Only inside people's homes | Nowhere very hot or very cold
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the cloning of Tyrannosaurus rex. | It is precluded, since the requisite DNA has not survived sixty-six million years | It has been accomplished in a number of laboratories, though not made public | It requires solely a mosquito preserved in amber | It is permitted exclusively in museums
exam: Identify the principal cause of the phenomenon designated coral bleaching. | The expulsion of symbiotic algae by corals in water of excessive temperature | The action of strong sunlight upon the pigment of the coral, which fades it | The deposition of salt upon the surface of the reef | The consumption of coral pigment by fish
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the international objective designated «30 by 30», agreed in 2022? | The protection of thirty per cent of land and sea by the year 2030 | The planting of thirty trees per person by the year 2030 | The establishment of thirty zoological gardens in every state | The rescue of thirty species within thirty years
exam: Ascertain the purpose of the facilities hereinafter referred to as frozen zoos. | The preservation in liquid nitrogen of cells, ova and sperm of rare animals | The keeping of living animals in refrigerated enclosures so as to arrest their ageing | The storage of food for animals in zoological gardens | The exhibition of ice sculptures of extinct species
